UNIT minimist
KIND FUNCTIONAL
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG parseArgs(args: string[], opts?: Options) -> ParsedArgs
    # DEFAULT EXPORT. The re-emitted module MUST `export default parseArgs`.
    # Options = {
    #   string?:  string | string[],
    #   boolean?: boolean | string | string[],
    #   alias?:   { [key: string]: string | string[] },
    #   default?: { [key: string]: any },
    #   stopEarly?: boolean,
    #   '--'?:    boolean,
    #   unknown?: (arg: string) => boolean      # FUNCTION — see SCOPE/out-of-contract
    # }
    # ParsedArgs = { _: any[], [flag: string]: any, '--'?: any[] }
DEPENDS-ON none   # zero runtime deps

# =============================================================================
# PROVENANCE
# =============================================================================
PROVENANCE minimist@1.2.8
  repo:    git://github.com/minimistjs/minimist  (homepage github.com/minimistjs/minimist)
  author:  James Halliday (substack); maintained by ljharb
  license: MIT
  source:  index.js (the package "main"); single file, 263 lines, 'use strict'
  All behavior below VERIFIED by executing the real package (node require + JSON.stringify).

# =============================================================================
# OVERVIEW / CONTRACT NOTES (read first — they govern the whole spec)
# =============================================================================
#
# OUTPUT-EQUALITY IS STRING-BASED. The grader compares JSON.stringify(encode(real))
#   against JSON.stringify(encode(reemit)). Therefore KEY INSERTION ORDER of the returned
#   object MATTERS. Reproduce the algorithm's mutation ORDER exactly, and store the result
#   as a PLAIN JS OBJECT (not Map). JS object key ordering rules then apply automatically:
#     * INTEGER-INDEX keys (canonical array-index strings: "0","1","2",... non-negative,
#       no leading zeros, < 2^32-1) are enumerated FIRST in ascending numeric order,
#       regardless of when inserted.
#     * All OTHER (string) keys follow, in INSERTION order.
#   Example: parseArgs(["--5","x","--b","1","--2","y"]) -> {"2":"y","5":"x","_":[],"b":1}
#   ("_" is inserted first but the integer keys "2","5" float ahead of it). A re-emitter that
#   builds a normal object via assignment in the order described below gets this for free.
#
# PURITY (VERIFIED): parseArgs does NOT mutate `args` or `opts`. The internal
#   `args = args.slice(...)` on a "--" terminator rebinds the LOCAL parameter only; the
#   caller's array is untouched (frozen input arrays parse fine). Defaults objects ARE shared
#   by REFERENCE into the output when a default value is an object (setKey assigns the same
#   reference); this is read-only w.r.t. parsing and benign for equality.
#
# `args` IS REQUIRED. There is NO process.argv fallback. If `args` is undefined/null (or any
#   value without an `.indexOf`/`.length`/index access), the function THROWS
#   (e.g. "Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'indexOf')"). `opts` is optional:
#   a falsy `opts` is replaced by {}.
#
# NUMBER COERCION uses isNumber(val) (defined below). When a value is coerced it becomes
#   Number(val) (a JS double). Large/duplicate-precision values follow IEEE-754 (e.g.
#   "99999999999999999999" -> 100000000000000000000). NaN/Infinity strings are NOT numbers
#   (isNumber returns false) so they stay strings.
#
# DETERMINISTIC THROWS ARE PART OF THE CONTRACT. Certain codec-faithful inputs make the REAL
#   minimist throw a (V8) TypeError; these are deterministic and reproducible by any re-emitter
#   that follows this algorithm on the same engine. The harness records them as {__throw:<msg>}
#   and grades them. See the THROW-CONTRACT section near the end.

# =============================================================================
# HELPERS (exact)
# =============================================================================

isNumber(x):
  # VERIFIED regex/logic, byte-exact from source:
  IF typeof x === 'number' -> return true
  IF /^0x[0-9a-f]+$/i .test(x)            -> return true     # hex, case-insensitive, REQUIRES >=1 hex digit
  return /^[-+]?(?:\d+(?:\.\d*)?|\.\d+)(e[-+]?\d+)?$/ .test(x)
  # Notes (all VERIFIED):
  #   "5"->T  "+5"->T  "-3"->T  "1.5"->T  ".5"->T  "5."->T  "1e3"->T  "1.5e-3"->T  "007"->T
  #   "0xff"->T  "0xFF"->T  "0x"->F (no digits)  ""->F  " 5 "->F (no trim)  "Infinity"->F  "NaN"->F
  #   The main regex's (e[-+]?\d+) group has NO /i flag and uses lowercase 'e' only, so an UPPERCASE
  #     'E' is not matched: "1E3" -> the optional group fails on 'E', the trailing "E3" then breaks the
  #     ^...$ anchor -> "1E3" is NOT a number (stays the string "1E3"). VERIFIED. ("1e3" IS a number.)

isConstructorOrProto(obj, key):
  return (key === 'constructor' && typeof obj[key] === 'function') || key === '__proto__'
  # PROTOTYPE-POLLUTION GUARD. Used inside setKey to refuse writing through these keys.

hasKey(obj, keys[]):
  # walk obj down keys[0..n-2] (each step: o = o[key] || {}); return (lastKey in o)
  o = obj
  for key in keys.slice(0, -1): o = o[key] || {}
  return (keys[last] in o)

# =============================================================================
# SETUP PHASE (build `flags`, `aliases`, `defaults`; seed booleans)
# =============================================================================

SETUP:
  if (!opts) opts = {}

  flags = { bools: {}, strings: {}, unknownFn: null }

  # --- unknown callback ---
  if typeof opts.unknown === 'function': flags.unknownFn = opts.unknown
    # opts.unknown is a FUNCTION -> documented OUT-OF-ENVELOPE for the generator (not codec-faithful).
    # Logic preserved here for completeness; see SCOPE.

  # --- boolean option ---
  if typeof opts.boolean === 'boolean' AND opts.boolean === true:
      flags.allBools = true                 # CATCH-ALL: treat every long --flag as boolean (see scan)
  else:
      # [].concat(opts.boolean) normalizes string|string[]|undefined -> array; .filter(Boolean)
      # drops falsy entries ("", null, undefined, 0, false). Each surviving key -> flags.bools[key]=true.
      for key in [].concat(opts.boolean).filter(Boolean): flags.bools[key] = true
      # NOTE: opts.boolean === false (a non-true boolean) takes the else branch and concats `false`,
      # which filter(Boolean) drops -> no bools. Same as omitting it.

  # --- alias normalization -> bidirectional map `aliases` (VERIFIED shape) ---
  aliases = {}
  for key in Object.keys(opts.alias || {}):
      aliases[key] = [].concat(opts.alias[key])          # canonical list for `key`
      for x in aliases[key]:
          # each alias target x points back to [key, ...(aliases[key] without x)]
          aliases[x] = [key].concat( aliases[key].filter(y => x !== y) )
  # EXAMPLES (VERIFIED):
  #   alias:{foo:"f"}      -> aliases = { foo:["f"], f:["foo"] }
  #   alias:{a:["b","c"]}  -> aliases = { a:["b","c"], b:["a","c"], c:["a","b"] }
  #   alias:{foo:["f","foo"]} (self-listed) -> the key "foo" appears in its own list; on a value set this
  #       DOUBLE-WRITES foo, yielding foo:[v,v] (a real edge — preserve literally).

  aliasIsBoolean(key):  # used during scan
      return aliases[key].some(x => flags.bools[x])

  # --- string option (suppresses number coercion AND boolean-ness for these keys) ---
  for key in [].concat(opts.string).filter(Boolean):
      flags.strings[key] = true
      if aliases[key]:
          for k in [].concat(aliases[key]): flags.strings[k] = true   # propagate string-ness to aliases
  # Special key "_" : opts.string includes "_" => positionals are NOT number-coerced (kept as strings).

  defaults = opts.default || {}

  argv = { _: [] }          # "_" inserted FIRST (see ordering note)

  # --- BOOLEAN SEED LOOP (runs BEFORE the scan) ---
  # For each declared boolean key, seed its value from defaults (or false if no default).
  for key in Object.keys(flags.bools):
      setArg(key, defaults[key] === undefined ? false : defaults[key])
      # setArg with no `arg` (3rd param undefined) => no unknown() gating; value passes through
      # isNumber check (false/<default> not coerced unless it's a numeric value/string).
      # This seeds bools (and their aliases via setArg) into argv in opts.boolean iteration order.

# =============================================================================
# argDefined / setKey / setArg (the write path)
# =============================================================================

argDefined(key, arg):
  return (flags.allBools && /^--[^=]+$/.test(arg))   # a long --flag (no '=') under catch-all
      || flags.strings[key]
      || flags.bools[key]
      || aliases[key]
  # Used only to decide whether unknownFn gets to veto. A "defined" key is NEVER vetoed.

setKey(obj, keys[], value):
  # DOTTED-KEY EXPANSION: keys is key.split('.'); walks/creates nested objects.
  o = obj
  for i in 0 .. keys.length-2:                # all but last
      k = keys[i]
      if isConstructorOrProto(o, k): RETURN          # pollution guard -> silently drop whole write
      if o[k] === undefined: o[k] = {}
      if o[k] === Object.prototype || o[k] === Number.prototype || o[k] === String.prototype: o[k] = {}
      if o[k] === Array.prototype: o[k] = []
      o = o[k]                                # NOTE: if o[k] is a SCALAR (string/number/boolean), the
                                              #   next iteration's `o[k2] = {}` assignment THROWS a
                                              #   TypeError (cannot create property on a primitive).
                                              #   See THROW-CONTRACT (dotted-collision).
  lastKey = keys[last]
  if isConstructorOrProto(o, lastKey): RETURN
  if o === Object.prototype || o === Number.prototype || o === String.prototype: o = {}
  if o === Array.prototype: o = []
  # ASSIGN / ACCUMULATE at o[lastKey]:
  if o[lastKey] === undefined OR flags.bools[lastKey] OR typeof o[lastKey] === 'boolean':
      o[lastKey] = value                              # set (also OVERWRITES an existing boolean)
  elif Array.isArray(o[lastKey]):
      o[lastKey].push(value)                          # append to existing array
  else:
      o[lastKey] = [o[lastKey], value]                # scalar collision -> wrap into [old, new]
  # CONSEQUENCES (VERIFIED):
  #   repeated --x 1 --x 2 --x 3 -> x = [1,2,3]
  #   dotted --a.b.c 1 -> a = { b: { c: 1 } }
  #   a key "." (single dot) splits to ["",""] -> nested { "": { "": value } }
  #   declared-boolean key: a later non-boolean set OVERWRITES (does not array-wrap) because flags.bools test.

setArg(key, val, arg):
  # 1) unknown() gate (only when `arg` is provided AND a custom unknownFn exists AND key not argDefined):
  if (arg AND flags.unknownFn AND !argDefined(key, arg)):
      if flags.unknownFn(arg) === false: RETURN       # veto: drop this key entirely (no alias writes)
  # 2) number coercion (suppressed by string-ness of THIS key):
  value = (!flags.strings[key] && isNumber(val)) ? Number(val) : val
  # 3) write primary key + every alias to the SAME value:
  setKey(argv, key.split('.'), value)
  for x in (aliases[key] || []): setKey(argv, x.split('.'), value)
  # ALIAS PROPAGATION: a single observed flag writes to its key AND all alias names, each via its
  # own dotted split, with the identical coerced value.
  # NOTE (THROW): `aliases[key]` is a plain-object lookup; for key === "__proto__" this resolves to
  #   Object.prototype, whose `.forEach` is undefined -> the `(aliases[key] || []).forEach(...)`
  #   call THROWS. See THROW-CONTRACT.

Object-prototype sentinel cases inside setKey (Object/Number/String/Array .prototype re-init) are only
  reachable through crafted polluted state and not via codec-faithful inputs; preserved verbatim above.

# =============================================================================
# "--" TERMINATOR PRE-SPLIT (before the scan)
# =============================================================================
TERMINATOR-SPLIT:
  if args.indexOf('--') !== -1:                       # FIRST occurrence only
      notFlags = args.slice(indexOf('--') + 1)         # everything AFTER the first "--"
      args     = args.slice(0, indexOf('--'))          # scan only the part BEFORE it (local rebind)
  else:
      notFlags = []
  # Disposition of notFlags happens at the very END (see FINALIZE). A SECOND "--" inside notFlags is
  # just data: parseArgs(["a","--","b","--","c"]) -> _ = ["a","b","--","c"] (default), or
  # with opts['--']: _=["a"], "--"=["b","--","c"].

# =============================================================================
# MAIN SCAN  (for i = 0; i < args.length; i++)  — branch order is EXACT
# =============================================================================
for i in 0 .. args.length-1:
  arg = args[i]

  # ---- BRANCH A: long "--key=value"  (regex /^--.+=/ , needs >=1 char before '=') ----
  if /^--.+=/.test(arg):
      m = arg.match(/^--([^=]+)=([\s\S]*)$/)           # [\s\S] so value may contain newlines
      key = m[1]; value = m[2]                          # value is everything after the FIRST '='
      if flags.bools[key]: value = (value !== 'false')  # declared-boolean: "false"->false else true
        # NOTE: this is ONLY for keys in flags.bools (explicit list), NOT for flags.allBools.
        # Under allBools, "--foo=bar" stays the string "bar".
      setArg(key, value, arg)
      # Edge: "--=x" does NOT match (/^--.+=/ needs >=1 char before '='; here that char is '=' itself
      #   at pos 2, nothing before) -> falls to BRANCH C, key="=x", -> "=x": true.
      # Edge: "--foo=a=b" -> key="foo", value="a=b".  "--foo=" -> key="foo", value="".

  # ---- BRANCH B: long negation "--no-KEY"  (regex /^--no-.+/) ----
  elif /^--no-.+/.test(arg):
      key = arg.match(/^--no-(.+)/)[1]
      setArg(key, false, arg)                           # always sets KEY (not "no-KEY") to false

  # ---- BRANCH C: long "--KEY" (no '=')  (regex /^--.+/) ----
  elif /^--.+/.test(arg):
      key  = arg.match(/^--(.+)/)[1]                    # e.g. "---" -> key "-", "--foo" -> "foo"
      next = args[i+1]
      if ( next !== undefined
           AND !/^(-|--)[^-]/.test(next)                # next is NOT another option-looking token
           AND !flags.bools[key]                        # KEY is not a declared boolean
           AND !flags.allBools                          # not catch-all boolean mode
           AND (aliases[key] ? !aliasIsBoolean(key) : true) ):   # KEY (via alias) not boolean
          setArg(key, next, arg); i += 1                # CONSUME next as the value
      elif /^(true|false)$/.test(next):
          setArg(key, next === 'true', arg); i += 1     # explicit boolean literal consumed
      else:
          setArg(key, flags.strings[key] ? '' : true, arg)   # bare flag -> true (or "" if string-typed)
      # LOOKAHEAD GUARD /^(-|--)[^-]/ : matches a token that starts with '-' or '--' followed by a
      #   NON-dash char. So "-3","--bar","-x" all MATCH (block consumption); but a LONE "-" does NOT
      #   match (only one char) -> "-" IS consumed as a value:  ["--foo","-"] -> foo:"-".
      #   ["--foo","-3"] -> foo:true (not consumed), and "-3" then parsed as its own short arg -> "3":true.

  # ---- BRANCH D: short cluster "-XYZ..."  (regex /^-[^-]+/ : leading '-' then >=1 non-dash) ----
  elif /^-[^-]+/.test(arg):
      letters = arg.slice(1, -1).split('')              # ALL chars except the leading '-' and the LAST char
      broken = false
      for j in 0 .. letters.length-1:
          next = arg.slice(j + 2)                        # the remainder of arg AFTER letters[j]

          # D1: remainder is exactly "-"  -> set this letter's value to "-", keep clustering
          if next === '-': setArg(letters[j], '-', arg); continue

          # D2: letter is [A-Za-z] AND remainder starts with '='  -> value = remainder after '='
          if /[A-Za-z]/.test(letters[j]) AND next[0] === '=':
              setArg(letters[j], next.slice(1), arg); broken = true; break   # e.g. -a=b -> a:"b"

          # D3: letter is [A-Za-z] AND remainder LOOKS NUMERIC  -> the whole remainder is the value
          if /[A-Za-z]/.test(letters[j]) AND /-?\d+(\.\d*)?(e-?\d+)?$/.test(next):
              setArg(letters[j], next, arg); broken = true; break
              # e.g. -n5 -> n:5 ; -n-3 -> n:-3 ; -n-3.5 -> n:-3.5  (then coerced by setArg/isNumber)
              # NOTE the regex is UNANCHORED at start (just `$` at end) -> "5", "-3", "3.5" tails match.

          # D4: the NEXT letter (letters[j+1]) is a NON-word char \W  -> take rest of arg as value
          if letters[j+1] AND letters[j+1].match(/\W/):
              setArg(letters[j], arg.slice(j + 2), arg); broken = true; break
              # e.g. -a-b-c : letters=["a","-","b","-"]; at j=0 letters[1]="-" is \W -> a := "-b-c".
              #      -a.b   : letters=["a","."]; at j=0 letters[1]="." is \W -> a := arg.slice(2)=".b".
          else:
              setArg(letters[j], flags.strings[letters[j]] ? '' : true, arg)   # plain bool letter

      # AFTER the cluster loop: handle the LAST char of arg
      key = arg.slice(-1)[0]
      if (!broken AND key !== '-'):
          if ( args[i+1]
               AND !/^(-|--)[^-]/.test(args[i+1])
               AND !flags.bools[key]
               AND (aliases[key] ? !aliasIsBoolean(key) : true) ):
              setArg(key, args[i+1], arg); i += 1       # last char consumes the NEXT argv token
          elif args[i+1] AND /^(true|false)$/.test(args[i+1]):
              setArg(key, args[i+1] === 'true', arg); i += 1
          else:
              setArg(key, flags.strings[key] ? '' : true, arg)
      # VERIFIED short-cluster examples:
      #   -abc        -> a:true,b:true,c:true
      #   -abc def    -> a:true,b:true,c:"def"  (last char 'c' consumes "def")
      #   -n5         -> n:5         -a.b -> a:".b"
      #   -a=b        -> a:"b"       -1abc -> "1":true,a:true,b:true,c:true  (leading digit is just a slot)
      #   -.5         -> "5":true, "":{"":true}  (letters=["."], '.' not [A-Za-z], split "." -> ["",""])
      #   -x.y.z=5    -> x:".y.z=5"  (D4 captures rest at j=0 since letters[1]='.' is \W)
      #   -a-         -> a:"-"  ;  -ab- -> a:true,b:"-"  ;  -a-b-c -> a:"-b-c"

  # ---- BRANCH E: everything else -> POSITIONAL ----
  else:
      if (!flags.unknownFn OR flags.unknownFn(arg) !== false):
          # push to _, number-coercing UNLESS opts.string includes "_"
          argv._.push( (flags.strings._ || !isNumber(arg)) ? arg : Number(arg) )
      if opts.stopEarly:
          argv._.push(...args.slice(i + 1))             # dump ALL remaining args verbatim (no parsing)
          break
      # Reaches here for: plain text, "" (empty string -> _ gets ""), lone "-" (matches NO option regex:
      #   /^-[^-]+/ needs a char AFTER '-', and "-" has none -> not BRANCH D -> positional "-"),
      #   pure numbers ("42" -> 42 unless string _).
      # stopEarly: the FIRST positional triggers the dump; flags BEFORE it parse normally.

# =============================================================================
# FINALIZE  (after the scan)
# =============================================================================
FINALIZE:
  # 1) apply DEFAULTS for any key still absent (dotted-aware via hasKey):
  for k in Object.keys(defaults):
      if !hasKey(argv, k.split('.')):
          setKey(argv, k.split('.'), defaults[k])
          for x in (aliases[k] || []): setKey(argv, x.split('.'), defaults[k])
      # Defaults are applied in opts.default iteration order, AFTER all scanned keys, so they appear
      # LAST in insertion order (subject to integer-key float). A present key (even if value false/0) is
      # NOT overwritten. Object-valued defaults are assigned BY REFERENCE (shared with opts.default).

  # 2) dispose of notFlags (the post-"--" tail):
  if opts['--']:
      argv['--'] = notFlags.slice()                     # a fresh COPY into a dedicated "--" array
  else:
      for k in notFlags: argv._.push(k)                 # appended to positionals (verbatim, unparsed)

  return argv

# =============================================================================
# CROSS-CUTTING SEMANTICS — quick reference (all VERIFIED)
# =============================================================================
# boolean (catch-all true):  every long --flag is boolean; "--foo bar" -> foo:true, "bar" positional;
#                            "--foo=bar" still keeps string "bar" (the '=' branch ignores allBools).
# boolean (list):            declared keys seed to false (or their default) up front; "--b value" -> b:true,
#                            "value" positional; "--b=false" -> b:false ; "--b=anything" -> b:true.
# string (list):             suppresses number coercion AND turns a bare flag into "" instead of true;
#                            propagates to aliases; opts.string:["_"] keeps positionals as strings.
# alias:                     bidirectional; one occurrence writes key + all aliases to the same value;
#                            boolean/string-ness propagates through aliases; aliasIsBoolean blocks
#                            value-consumption when ANY alias of the key is a declared boolean.
# default:                   seeds bools early; fills absent keys (and their aliases) at the end; never
#                            overwrites a present key; dotted defaults use hasKey for presence.
# stopEarly:                 on the FIRST positional, remaining args are dumped verbatim into _.
# '--' (opt true):           post-"--" tail captured in argv['--'] (copy) instead of _.
# numeric positionals:       coerced via isNumber unless opts.string includes "_".
# prototype pollution:       setKey refuses to write through "__proto__" or a function-valued
#                            "constructor"; the whole write is dropped (returns silently).

# =============================================================================
# THROW-CONTRACT (deterministic throws on codec-faithful inputs — gradable as {__throw})
# =============================================================================
# All VERIFIED; messages are stable on a given V8/Node. A re-emitter implementing this algorithm
# verbatim reproduces both the throw AND its message.
#
# (T1) DOTTED-KEY COLLISION onto a non-object value. If a key (or alias) writes a SCALAR
#      (string / number / boolean) to some path, and a LATER dotted key tries to descend THROUGH that
#      scalar, setKey's `o[k2] = {}` (or the final assignment) throws a TypeError:
#        "Cannot create property '<seg>' on <type> '<value>'"
#      Examples (VERIFIED):
#        ["--a.b","--a.b.c"]    -> a:=true (BRANCH C bare), then descend into true -> THROW
#                                  "Cannot create property 'c' on boolean 'true'"
#        ["--a.b=v","--a.b.c"]  -> "Cannot create property 'c' on string 'v'"
#        ["-a-","--a.b.c"]      -> a:="-" then descend -> "Cannot create property 'b' on string '-'"
#      Also reachable when the COLLISION happens at the `in` check inside hasKey during FINALIZE
#      defaults (e.g. a dotted default whose parent path is a scalar):
#        "Cannot use 'in' operator to search for '<seg>' in <value>"
#
# (T2) "__proto__" AS A BARE LONG KEY. ["--__proto__"] / ["--__proto__","y"] reach setArg with
#      key="__proto__"; `aliases["__proto__"]` resolves to Object.prototype (not undefined), and
#      `(Object.prototype).forEach` is undefined -> THROW:
#        "(aliases[key] || []).forEach is not a function"
#      (By contrast a DOTTED proto key like "--__proto__.x" is caught by isConstructorOrProto and the
#       write is dropped -> { _: [] }, no throw. "--constructor.x" likewise dropped.)
#
# These throw paths ARE exercised by the curated prefix (a controlled handful) so the oracle pins
# them; the random fill is shaped to AVOID accidentally over-producing dotted-collision throws (it
# never mixes a scalar dotted key with a deeper dotted key on the same root), keeping the held-out
# dominated by the rich non-throwing parsing surface.

# =============================================================================
# ORACLE
# =============================================================================
ORACLE value -> oracle.json
  # Pure deterministic function. Expecteds are stamped by executing the real minimist@1.2.8 on the
  # generator's inputs (throwing inputs recorded as {__throw:<msg>}). The returned object is compared
  # by JSON.stringify(encode(.)) so KEY ORDER is part of the contract (reproduce mutation order +
  # plain-object semantics as specified above).

# =============================================================================
# SCOPE
# =============================================================================
SCOPE
  IN-CONTRACT LOGIC (reproduce exactly):
    - The full setup/scan/finalize pipeline above: isNumber, isConstructorOrProto, hasKey, setKey
      (dotted expansion + array accumulation + boolean overwrite + pollution guard), setArg
      (number coercion + alias propagation), argDefined, aliasIsBoolean.
    - Alias-map normalization (bidirectional), boolean seeding, string-ness propagation, defaults.
    - All five scan branches A..E with EXACT regexes, lookahead guards, and short-cluster sub-rules.
    - "--" terminator split and the opts['--'] vs _ disposition; stopEarly dump.
    - Output object KEY INSERTION ORDER (built as a plain JS object).
    - The deterministic THROW-CONTRACT (T1, T2).

  CARRIED DATA: none. (No large literal tables; all constants are inline regexes/strings already
    transcribed above verbatim.)

  OUT-OF-CONTRACT / OUT-OF-ENVELOPE (documented, NOT emitted by genInputs):
    - opts.unknown is a FUNCTION (predicate (arg)->boolean). Not codec-faithful (functions don't
      survive JSON round-trip), so the generator NEVER emits opts.unknown. Its behavior is fully
      specified above (unknownFn gate in setArg + the BRANCH-E positional gate): a custom predicate
      returning false VETOES an unknown (not argDefined) arg — dropping that key/positional entirely.
      A blind re-emitter reconstructs this from the spec; it is simply never exercised by the oracle.
    - Calling with a missing/undefined/null/non-array `args` THROWS (no process.argv fallback). The
      generator never emits such inputs (always an array first arg). Documented as the throw contract.
    - Object-valued opts.default sharing by reference into the output (read-only; equality-safe).
    - Object/Array.prototype sentinel re-init inside setKey: only reachable via crafted polluted state;
      not reachable through codec-faithful inputs, documented for completeness.
